TESTDATA["pet8-t4"] = {"title":"Cambridge PET 8 · Test 4","format":"2010","reading":[{"part":1,"items":[{"n":1,"text":"John\nI've seen an advert for a new French restaurant in the town centre. Have you eaten there? Would it be suitable for Mum's birthday on Saturday?\nJulie","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Julie is asking John if French food is available at a new restaurant.","Julie is inviting John to a birthday party in the town centre.","Julie wants John's advice about a venue for a family celebration."],"answer":"C"},{"n":2,"text":"Olympia Sportswear\nFurther discounts available on certain sale items. Check label for details.","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Customers can get discounts on all goods in the sale.","Customers can only get reduced prices on goods with labels.","Customers can get special prices on some goods."],"answer":"C"},{"n":3,"text":"To: Bill\nFrom: Sonia\nHi\nI have to let the theatre know how many tickets are needed. Have you decided yet whether to come with us or not? Hope so!","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"Sonia has written the email to","options":["find out if Bill wants a theatre ticket.","tell Bill how many theatre tickets they need.","remind Bill to get some theatre tickets."],"answer":"A"},{"n":4,"text":"Matt,\nSunday's race starts at 10.00. We can pick up our numbers at registration one hour before. There are no changing facilities so we must arrive ready to run.\nJo","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Jo wants Matt to pick her up an hour before the race starts.","Jo is reminding Matt to put on his running kit before he leaves home.","Jo is expecting Matt to collect their race numbers from registration before she arrives."],"answer":"B"},{"n":5,"text":"COMFORTABLE SOFA £20\ntoo large for my new apartment\nBuyer must collect\nCall Andre: 298354","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Andre wants to buy a large sofa for his new apartment.","Andre will sell his sofa as long as it is taken away.","Buyers should call Andre to arrange transport for the sofa."],"answer":"B"}]},{"part":2,"people":[{"n":6,"name":"Yang","text":"Yang is looking for used copies of modern novels for her book group. She would like to visit a bookshop where her two young daughters will be welcome, with drinks and snacks available.","answer":"G"},{"n":7,"name":"Stefan","text":"Stefan loves beautiful art books but would like to check what's available on the Internet before visiting. He wants to find a bookshop where he can get lunch and spend the afternoon looking at books.","answer":"E"},{"n":8,"name":"Scott","text":"Scott has been unable to get a particular textbook which he needs urgently for his project on health care for the elderly. He is very busy and would prefer to have it delivered.","answer":"F"},{"n":9,"name":"Jasmin","text":"Jasmin is setting up a library of classic fiction for her primary school. Before buying some books online, she would like to go to the shop and discuss suitable books with staff.","answer":"D"},{"n":10,"name":"Ian","text":"Ian wants a quiet bookshop where he can relax in comfort with a coffee for a few hours after a long day at work. He'd like to get a few cheap novels to take home.","answer":"B"}],"texts":[{"letter":"A","title":"Bookworm","text":"This shop has used books for all tastes but customers will have to visit in person and look around as Bookworm does not have a website. The best sections are on medicine, cookery and art. These include some rare and expensive books."},{"letter":"B","title":"Bales","text":"This shop is famous for its range of titles, from college textbooks to classic literature, and is known for its peaceful atmosphere. Regular customers come for the excellent café with its sofas and armchairs, which stays open until late."},{"letter":"C","title":"Westwood's","text":"There's no room to sit down in this tiny back-street bookshop, and the owner is often too busy to chat. On the first floor are children's books and fine art books for collectors. A separate level there are less expensive general fiction hardbacks."},{"letter":"D","title":"Topping's","text":"You'll only find good-quality used hardback books here on certain subjects. Topping's is run by three experts in art, travel and literature for the young. They are happy to chat and advise customers. The shelves are well-organised, and books can also be ordered from the shop's website."},{"letter":"E","title":"Scrimshaw's","text":"Scrimshaw's has a huge selection of books showing the works of the great painters. Most are in excellent condition and reasonably priced, and the store's online guide is regularly updated. The basement also has a café and a large number of out-of-date school textbooks on sale."},{"letter":"F","title":"Regal","text":"Here you'll find the country's largest selection of science-related books, including topics such as psychology and medicine. There are 70,000 used and new books, all at discount prices. Regal also has an efficient online ordering service plus comfortable sofas and freshly made coffee, and is open during office hours."},{"letter":"G","title":"Hol's","text":"Although its prices are high, the fun of Hol's is its lively atmosphere. It's in an old railway station, where you can enjoy afternoon tea and cake in the waiting room. Or leave the kids to play in the former ticket office while you look at the huge selection of recent fiction."},{"letter":"H","title":"Wenlock's","text":"This prize-winning bookshop is noted for its wide selection of titles, both paperback and hardback. Fiction is downstairs, non-fiction upstairs under the ancient roof of this 15th century building. The book club welcomes new members to discuss a different novel one evening each month."}]},{"part":3,"title":"Arctic expedition","text":"Ann Daniels and Helen Findlay faced the dangers of polar bears, thin ice and frostbite when they became members of an expedition to the Arctic in 2010.\n\nThe expedition team consisted of six scientists and a separate group of three explorers. While Helen and the other scientists were setting up a laboratory site on the ice in northern Canada, Ann and two other explorers set off on a 500 kilometre journey on foot across floating sea ice from Greenland to the North Pole.\n\nBoth groups planned to measure how thick the ice was at various sites in the Arctic. They would also collect water samples for testing, in order to discover how the chemistry of sea water was changing, and what effect this was having on plant-life and animals in the ocean. On previous expeditions, the water was tested in the summer, using ships at sea, but they had never collected it from under the ice before. The research was important for understanding what might happen in other oceans around the world in the future, as a result of climate change.\n\nAnn Daniels was working in a bank in 1996 when she answered an advert, inviting ordinary women to join a trek to the North Pole in the Arctic. Since then she has become one of the world's leading professional polar explorers. For the 2010 expedition, she and her two colleagues attended a week-long fitness camp to prepare them for pulling the 120 kilogram sledges over the ice for 12 hours a day.\n\nDespite her previous experience, however, Ann suffered in the freezing conditions. 'It gets into your bones and never leaves you, even when you're in your tent at night,' she said. 'Especially at the end of an expedition when you're totally exhausted.' Yet Ann successfully guided her companions for 73 days and collected all the samples and information that were needed.\n\nHelen Findlay was one of the scientists on the laboratory site based in Canada. Although it was not her first visit to the Arctic, she had never been there in winter and realised that carrying out experiments in such an extreme environment would be challenging. 'The laboratory tents were heated, but getting equipment to work outside while wearing gloves and thick clothes meant every job took twice as long as it would normally. When they went out onto the ice to collect samples, the scientists were protected by two guides with guns and flares to frighten any curious polar bears who might be attracted by the smell of humans.'\n\nDespite the difficulties, both Ann and Helen stayed out on the ice for the whole of the project, and the results of their studies have been widely published.","items":[{"n":11,"q":"A building was ready for the scientists to work in on their arrival in northern Canada.","answer":"B"},{"n":12,"q":"This was the first time scientists had tested Arctic sea water during the winter months.","answer":"A"},{"n":13,"q":"Information was collected on the expedition to help explain the reasons for climate change.","answer":"B"},{"n":14,"q":"The North Pole was the destination of a previous expedition.","answer":"A"},{"n":15,"q":"Ann had to do special training to be strong enough for the expedition.","answer":"A"},{"n":16,"q":"During her career, Ann has discovered ways to deal with feeling cold.","answer":"B"},{"n":17,"q":"Ann's group achieved everything they had aimed to do.","answer":"A"},{"n":18,"q":"Helen knew it would be difficult doing experiments in freezing conditions.","answer":"A"},{"n":19,"q":"When she went onto the ice, Helen carried equipment to prevent wild animals from attacking.","answer":"B"},{"n":20,"q":"The expedition research is now available for other scientists to read.","answer":"A"}]},{"part":4,"title":"Helen Skelton","text":"Television presenter Helen Skelton has completed various dangerous challenges for charity, including a desert marathon run and a journey down the Amazon river in a tiny boat. For her latest challenge, Helen walked on a high wire, a long thin piece of metal, 66 metres above the ground, between the two towers of an empty power station. There was no safety net, only a belt around her waist attached to another wire above. To help her keep her balance and not to fall, she carried a long stick, weighing eight kilograms.\n\nHelen took lessons from an expert high-wire walker. Although she already had a few circus skills, she quickly discovered that high-wire walking was completely different. 'The training was a shock', said Helen. 'I didn't realise how strong I'd need to be. Every day I started at 8 am with a run up a steep hill; then came an hour of exercises, with another hour of them after my day practising on the wire. I also had to learn how to fall onto the wire so that if I slipped I could carry on. Every time I fell on the wire it hurt badly, but it was essential to keep a positive attitude: if I was nervous and started shaking, so would the wire!'\n\nThe day of the actual challenge was windy and Helen controlled herself by taking deep breaths as she moved step by step along the wire. The 150-metre walk took 15 minutes. 'That was the scariest thing I've done,' she admitted afterwards. 'I'm glad it's over!'\n\nThe experience has not prevented Helen, however, from planning her next charity project: to reach the South Pole by ski, kite and bike.","items":[{"n":21,"q":"What is the writer doing in this text?","options":["introducing a new television presenter","getting support for a charity project","describing a woman's achievement","recommending a television programme"],"answer":"C"},{"n":22,"q":"Helen knew she would be safe if she fell because","options":["she had a net that could catch her.","she had a stick to support herself with.","she was wearing special equipment.","she was quite close to the ground."],"answer":"C"},{"n":23,"q":"During her training, Helen was surprised to find that","options":["staying on the wire was so difficult.","she had to work so hard at her fitness.","it was so important to be confident.","her circus skills were so useful."],"answer":"B"},{"n":24,"q":"When Helen performed her walk on the high wire","options":["she was sorry to get to the end.","the weather conditions were perfect.","it took less time than expected.","she managed to keep herself calm."],"answer":"D"},{"n":25,"q":"What might Helen say about the walk?","options":["The view from the high wire was fantastic – I loved every minute of it. I can't wait to have another go.","I must admit I was a little bit frightened, but it wasn't nearly as bad as going down the Amazon on my own.","That was the longest quarter of an hour of my life. I couldn't breathe at all when I first stepped onto the wire.","The training could be painful at times but at least I'm in really good condition now, ready for my next challenge."],"answer":"D"}]},{"part":5,"title":"Finding Gold","text":"Most people would (0) ……… (agree) that finding the perfect ring for your partner is difficult. However, John Greenwood, a Scottish engineer, [26] himself an extra challenge. [27] of taking a trip to the jeweller's, John [28] much of last year standing up to his knees in freezing water. He was looking for tiny pieces of gold [29] the stones on the river beds in the Scottish Highlands.\n\nUsing very [30] equipment, John worked hard to [31] enough gold to make not just an engagement ring for his girlfriend, Morag, [32] also their two wedding rings. 'I was out there [33] weekend and in the evenings before it got dark, in freezing cold water,' he said.\n\nExperts think that John was amazingly lucky to find so much gold in such a [34] time. Many people [35] for 30 years without getting anything.","gaps":{"26":{"options":["did","made","gave","had"],"answer":"C"},"27":{"options":["Rather","Besides","Along","Instead"],"answer":"D"},"28":{"options":["took","paid","spent","held"],"answer":"C"},"29":{"options":["among","towards","against","through"],"answer":"A"},"30":{"options":["easy","plain","necessary","basic"],"answer":"D"},"31":{"options":["choose","collect","pick","select"],"answer":"B"},"32":{"options":["or","and","but","even"],"answer":"C"},"33":{"options":["every","some","most","any"],"answer":"A"},"34":{"options":["little","short","quick","small"],"answer":"B"},"35":{"options":["reach","follow","search","keep"],"answer":"C"}}}],"writing":[{"part":1,"title":"Here are some sentences about a train journey.","items":[{"n":1,"first":"It was several months since Jessica last went on a train.","before":"Jessica hadn't been on a train","after":"several months.","answers":["for"]},{"n":2,"first":"Jessica bought her ticket in advance online.","before":"Jessica's ticket","after":"bought in advance online.","answers":["was"]},{"n":3,"first":"It wasn't easy to find a seat because the train was crowded.","before":"The train was","after":"crowded that it was difficult to find a seat.","answers":["so","that"]},{"n":4,"first":"Jessica asked what time they would arrive in York.","before":"Jessica asked 'What time","after":"we arrive in York?'","answers":["will","should","do"]},{"n":5,"first":"The journey wasn't boring for Jessica because she had a good book.","before":"Jessica","after":"find the journey boring because she had a good book.","answers":["did not","didn't"]}]},{"part":2,"items":[{"n":6,"kind":"message","task":"You and your friend, Jerry, have tickets for a football match at the weekend, but now you cannot go.\n\nWrite a note to Jerry. In your note, you should\n• apologise to Jerry\n• explain why you cannot go\n• suggest what Jerry could do with your ticket.\n\nWrite 35–45 words on your answer sheet.","points":["scusarti con Jerry","spiegare perché non puoi andare","suggerire che cosa Jerry potrebbe fare con il tuo biglietto"]}]},{"part":3,"items":[{"n":7,"kind":"letter","task":"This is part of a letter you receive from a friend.\n\n'I can't decide whether to buy a new bicycle from a shop or on the Internet. Where do you think I should buy it? Is it better to buy some things from shops? Do you buy things on the Internet?'\n\nNow write a letter answering your friend's questions.\nWrite your letter in about 100 words on your answer sheet."},{"n":8,"kind":"story","task":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story.\nYour story must begin with this sentence:\n\nI was amazed when I opened the bag.\n\nWrite your story in about 100 words on your answer sheet."}]}],"listening":[{"part":1,"audio":"audio/pet8-t4-l1.mp3","items":[{"n":1,"q":"Where will Peter give Joanna the book?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"q":"What did the girl buy at the market?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":3,"q":"What will the weather be like for the race?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":4,"q":"What does the girl like best about the city?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":5,"q":"What does the boy think was unusual about the film?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":6,"q":"What happened during the football match?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":7,"q":"Which part of the gallery did the man visit?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"}],"example":"img/pet8-t4-l1-ex.jpg"},{"part":2,"intro":"You will hear an interview with a woman called Amy Rowntree, who works as a fashion designer.","audio":"audio/pet8-t4-l2.mp3","items":[{"n":8,"q":"Amy decided to become a fashion designer while","options":["she was still at school.","she was working in a shop.","she was helping run a fashion show."],"answer":"B"},{"n":9,"q":"Amy says the most important skill for a designer is","options":["remaining patient in difficult situations.","knowing about different materials.","understanding how clothes are made."],"answer":"C"},{"n":10,"q":"What does Amy particularly like about her work?","options":["being able to sell her designs","seeing her clothes on display","having her name recognised"],"answer":"C"},{"n":11,"q":"How does Amy feel about today's fashion?","options":["She would like clothes to be more practical.","She feels clothes should be more attractive.","She's pleased sports clothes are popular."],"answer":"B"},{"n":12,"q":"What helps Amy find new ideas for designs?","options":["seeing things around her.","going to visit museums.","looking at photos of clothes."],"answer":"A"},{"n":13,"q":"What project is Amy working on at the moment?","options":["a collection of clothes for a film","a fashion show in New York","a new range for a London store"],"answer":"A"}]},{"part":3,"intro":"You will hear a student called Steve talking about working in a forest in New Zealand.","audio":"audio/pet8-t4-l3.mp3","notes":"Working in a forest in New Zealand\nSteve had accommodation in a shared [14].\nSteve says it was important to have good [15] at the end of each day.\nSteve worked with a team that made a [16] through the forest.\nSteve also collected information needed for a [17] of the forest area.\nIn the morning Steve was surprised to hear the sound of [18] in the forest.\nWhen he did the trip, Steve only had to pay for his [19].","answers":{"14":["tent","a tent","tents"],"15":["meals"],"16":["path","the path","paths"],"17":["map","a map","maps"],"18":["rabbits","rabbit"],"19":["flight","flights","the flight"]}},{"part":4,"intro":"You will hear a conversation between a woman called Martha and a man called James about a TV series called Medicine, which is about a doctor who works in a hospital.","audio":"audio/pet8-t4-l4.mp3","items":[{"n":20,"q":"They agree that the last show in the series was complicated.","answer":"A"},{"n":21,"q":"James believes that the series is successful because of the main character.","answer":"A"},{"n":22,"q":"They both admire the main character's behaviour.","answer":"B"},{"n":23,"q":"Martha thinks that the main character has similar skills to a detective.","answer":"A"},{"n":24,"q":"James was surprised to find the main character so funny.","answer":"B"},{"n":25,"q":"They both plan to watch the series again.","answer":"B"}]}]};
